{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"startup-wind-down-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Startup wind-down track","description":"Winds down a startup or small company in gated steps - confirm the decision, cash and creditors, staff, customers and assets, then the closing paperwork to verify with professionals.","category":"entrepreneurship","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","operate","ship"],"role":["founder","executive"],"advice_risk":["legal","financial"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","notes","dataset"],"output":["plan","checklist","message"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["shutdown","company-closure","creditors","insolvency-check","orderly-exit"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["evaluate-pivot","plan-cofounder-breakup","write-investor-update","plan-layoff-conversation"],"personas":["startup-mentor","small-business-advisor"]},"args":[{"name":"company","description":"The company - what it does, country of registration, legal form, staff and contractors, customers and contracts, investors, and why you are considering closing.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"finances","description":"Cash in the bank, monthly burn, money owed to the company, debts and money owed to others (suppliers, tax, staff, loans, leases), and any personal guarantees.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"decision","file":"steps/01-decision.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"cash-and-creditors","file":"steps/02-cash-and-creditors.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"people","file":"steps/03-people.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"customers-and-assets","file":"steps/04-customers-and-assets.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"closing","file":"steps/05-closing.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Confirm the decision","Cash and creditors","Staff and contractors","Customers, contracts and assets","Closing paperwork","Running checklist"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Takes a founder through closing a company in an orderly, honest way. Each step stops for approval.\n\n<company>\n{{company}}\n</company>\n\n<finances>\n{{finances}}\n</finances>\n\nRules for every step:\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- The order in which debts are paid, directors' duties once a company may be insolvent, staff entitlements and the formal closure route depend on the country. Present them as checks with an accountant, an insolvency practitioner or a lawyer; never state the rule.\n- If the company may not be able to pay its debts in full, say early that directors' duties can change and that advice should come before paying any creditor, founder or investor.\n- Never suggest moving assets out, favouring some creditors, or hiding the closure from people owed money.\n- Keep a running checklist (Item | Owner | Deadline | Status), reprinted at the end of each step.","steps":[{"id":"decision","file":"steps/01-decision.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Confirm the decision\n\n1. Summarise why closing is on the table and the evidence.\n2. List the alternatives still open and whether each is realistic: a pivot, a sale or acqui-hire of the team or assets, merging, handing the product to a customer or community, a bridge from investors, or going dormant. For each, the time and cash it needs against the runway.\n3. Estimate the cash needed for an orderly close (final payroll and entitlements as placeholders, notice periods, contract exits, professional fees, data and records) and compare with cash on hand. If the cash will not cover debts, flag the solvency question for urgent professional advice.\n4. Set the decision deadline and who must agree (cofounders, board, investors as the documents require).\n\nStop for the founder to confirm the decision to wind down, or choose an alternative."},{"id":"cash-and-creditors","file":"steps/02-cash-and-creditors.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Cash and creditors\n\n1. List everything owed by the company: staff, tax, suppliers, landlord, loans, leases, customer prepayments and refunds, investor instruments. Mark any personal guarantees.\n2. List everything owed to the company and how to collect it.\n3. Build a week-by-week cash plan to the closing date.\n4. Questions for the accountant or insolvency practitioner: is the company solvent, which closure route fits, what order of payment applies, and what directors must and must not do now.\n5. Stop new spending and commitments except what the close needs.\n\nStop for approval. Ask the founder to get the solvency and route answer before step 3 pays anyone."},{"id":"people","file":"steps/03-people.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Staff and contractors\n\n1. List each person with role, contract type, notice period and entitlements to check (final pay, holiday pay, severance, benefits, equity options). Mark unknowns.\n2. Consultation or notice duties to check with an employment adviser before announcing.\n3. Plan the conversations: who tells whom, when, and in what order, ideally all staff on the same day; a short honest script; and how you will help them find work (references, introductions, time off for interviews).\n4. Knowledge handover needed before people leave.\n\nStop for approval of the people plan before anyone is told."},{"id":"customers-and-assets","file":"steps/04-customers-and-assets.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Customers, contracts and assets\n\n1. Customers: notice periods in contracts, refunds for prepaid services, a data export for each customer, and a shutdown date. Draft a short, clear customer notice.\n2. Contracts: leases, software subscriptions, supplier and partner agreements, with termination terms and dates.\n3. Assets: equipment, stock, code, domains, trademarks, customer lists. Note that selling assets, especially personal data, needs proper value and may need consent; check with the professional.\n4. Data: what to delete, what to keep for legal record-keeping periods, and who holds the records.\n5. Investors: a draft update explaining the decision, what happens to remaining cash, and the timeline.\n\nStop for approval of the notices and the asset plan."},{"id":"closing","file":"steps/05-closing.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Closing paperwork\n\n1. Final accounts and tax returns, deregistration for taxes and payroll, and closing bank and payment accounts after the final payments clear.\n2. The formal closure application or process for the route chosen, with the professional who handles it.\n3. Records to keep and for how long, and who keeps them.\n4. Personal follow-up for the founder: guarantees released, personal tax questions, and time to recover.\n\nOutput the final checklist with every item's status and anything still open."}]}
